---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can a cancer survivor get burial insurance?',
    a: '<p>Yes. Many survivors qualify for full coverage from day one once a few years have passed since treatment ended. Before then, a graded or guaranteed issue plan is the usual choice.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does skin cancer count?',
    a: '<p>Basal cell and squamous cell skin cancers usually don’t. Melanoma usually does. Each insurer words its questions differently, so ask us how a particular insurer treats your history.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my cancer is in remission?',
    a: '<p>Insurers mostly care about when treatment ended. Tell us the date your last treatment finished, whether that was surgery, chemotherapy, radiation or a medicine you take every day.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you need my medical records?',
    a: '<p>Usually not. Final expense insurers rely on the health questions and, with your permission, prescription and medical database checks. There’s no exam.</p>',
  },
];
---
<Guide
  title="Burial Insurance After Cancer: What to Expect"
  h1="Burial insurance after cancer"
  description="Final expense insurance after cancer: how far back insurers look, what counts, which plan is typical during and after treatment, and what to have ready."
  path="/burial-insurance-after-cancer/"
  crumb="Burial insurance after cancer"
  eyebrow="Health conditions"
  lede="If you've had cancer, the key question is when your treatment ended. Many people who are a few years past treatment qualify for full coverage."
  published="2026-11-07"
  reviewed="2026-11-07"
  summary={[
    'During treatment, or soon after, the usual choice is a plan with a waiting period.',
    'Many insurers look back two to five years after treatment ends; after that, full coverage is often possible.',
    "Most insurers don't count basal cell or squamous cell skin cancer.",
    'No medical exam. Approval depends on your answers to a few health questions.',
  ]}
>
  <h2>How insurers look at cancer</h2>
  <p>
    The health questions usually ask whether you've been diagnosed with or treated for cancer within a set time, commonly two, three or five
    years. Treatment usually includes surgery, chemotherapy, radiation and some medicines taken afterward. Some insurers also ask whether a
    cancer has come back or spread.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many insurers leave out basal cell and squamous cell skin cancers, the most common and least serious kinds. Some medicines taken for
    years after treatment, such as hormone therapy after breast cancer, may count as ongoing treatment with some insurers, so mention them.
  </p>

  <h2>What's typical, by situation</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>Typical outcomes after cancer. Each insurer sets its own rules, so this is a starting point, not a promise.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Your situation</th><th scope="col">What's typical</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">In treatment now</th><td data-label="What's typical">Usually a guaranteed issue plan, with a waiting period.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Treatment ended in the last two years</th><td data-label="What's typical">Often a graded or guaranteed issue plan.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Treatment ended two to five years ago</th><td data-label="What's typical">Depends on the insurer; some offer full coverage from day one.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cancer-free for five years or more</th><td data-label="What's typical">Often full coverage from day one, if the rest of your health allows it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cancer that came back or spread</th><td data-label="What's typical">Often a plan with a waiting period; it depends on timing.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What helps your application</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The type of cancer</strong> and the date it was diagnosed.</li>
    <li><strong>The date your last treatment ended,</strong> including surgery, chemotherapy, radiation or daily medicines.</li>
    <li><strong>A list of the medicines you take now,</strong> with doses.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Honest answers.</strong> During the first two years, an insurer can review the application if there's a claim, and a wrong
      answer can lead to a denied claim.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If you're in treatment now</h2>
  <p>
    A guaranteed issue plan accepts you without health questions. If death from natural causes happens during the waiting period, usually the
    first two or three years, the insurer refunds the premiums paid, plus interest, instead of paying the full amount. Accidental death is
    usually covered in full from the start. <a href="/guaranteed-issue-vs-simplified-issue/">How the plan types work</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If someone has been told their illness is terminal, a guaranteed issue plan may still accept them, but because of the waiting period it
    may not pay the full amount. We'll tell you honestly whether it makes sense, or whether money set aside would serve your family better.
  </p>
  <p>Once treatment is behind you and time passes, ask us whether a full-coverage plan has opened up. Never cancel a policy until the new one is approved and in force.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h3>Helping a parent after a cancer diagnosis?</h3>
    <p>Your parent answers the health questions and signs, and you can often pay. <a href="/burial-insurance-for-parents/">How buying for a parent works</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Questions about cancer and burial insurance</h2>
  <Faq items={faqs} />
</Guide>
